      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T22:42:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In short, it is essentially the same thing, except using the Zephyr API.\nThe flags here are OR\u0027ed with the flags in the spec that is taken from the device tree GPIO flags.\n\nIn long:\nHere we also have an example of where our use of the standard Zephyr GPIO API is a bit of a mismatch.\nThe standard Zephyr gpio API (like this call) uses a struct gpio_dt_spec to hold the details of the GPIO (controller, pin, flags), but the flags are only 8 bits.\nIn our definition of the GPIO in the named-gpio node, we include flags that are outside the 8 bits reserved, and so we need to include these flags in the setup (which as I mentioned the flags in this call are \u0027extra\u0027, and OR\u0027ed in with the 8 bits of flags in the gpio_dt_spec).\nThe idea is that the 8 bits should capture the physical characteristics of the pin, such as voltaqe, pull-up/pull-down etc. Everything else is settable by the code. Such as GPIO_INPUT.\n\nIn our common init, we actually save the complete 32 bits of flags from the named-gpio GPIO definition, and use that to set the initial state of the GPIO.\nI was torn about using gpio_reset, because that is part of the old GPIO API, not part of the Zephyr API.",
      "parentUuid": "4bf48a52_69df948e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 187,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 188,
        "endChar": 21
