      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T16:53:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This raises a question. The hibernate wake pin doesn\u0027t need to have an interrupt handler. It just wakes the EC up and done. The wake is like a power on and follows a normal boot process. The interrupt handle won\u0027t be triggered. IMO, the wake pins are likely to bind to a GPIO, instead of an interrupt.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "77292f7c_e1bb1b39",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T22:42:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the problem here is that the previous code was broken anyway, and this code\ndoesn\u0027t do anything to fix that (mainly because I didn\u0027t know the internals of how wake was implemented), and the best I thought I could do is put in a TODO to flag the breakage.\n\nThe code was broken because it was calling gpio_enable_interrupt on a GPIO signal that had no corresponding entry in EC_CROS_GPIO_INTERRUPTS. The effect was that the gpio_enable_interrupt was ignored (this is different behaviour in Zephyr from the old EC-OS). And it\u0027s the same thing with gpio_rtc_ec_wake_odl.\nSo in effect this pin was being completely ignored as a wake signal, so I made it obvious.\nFixing this seemed out of the scope of this change.",
      "parentUuid": "cc989a4e_9b80b8d3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "66f7a562_ea310fa1",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T22:50:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So to separately answer whether it should bind to a GPIO instead of using an interrupt, this is certainly possible, and likely a more correct solution.\n\nSo the code (as it was) was broken because it was assuming it could enable the interrupt for any GPIO (which isn\u0027t true, at least not while calling gpio_enable_interrupt or gpio_enable_dt_interrupt). If these calls are used, you can only enable interrupts if the GPIO has been previously configured as an interrupt source - the old code by being one of the EC_CROS_GPIO_INTERRUPTS, or in the new code by having a node in the gpio-interrupts node.\n\nIt\u0027s likely the correct resolution here that will also fix the underlying problem is to avoid using gpio_enable_dt_interrupt altogether, and instead have the wake setup code use the core Zephyr interrupt API to setup a callback and enable the interrupt. Then I agree the binding should be to a GPIO, not the interrupt.\nI\u0027ll do that.",
      "parentUuid": "77292f7c_e1bb1b39",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1d1b7b30_a932df3b",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-28T23:26:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the problem with trying to get the interrupt setup as a completely\ninternal configuration in the wake setup code is that this code does not know about the interrupt configuration of the pins i.e edge falling/both etc.\nThis is what you get when you use an interrupt node.\nSo my suggestion is that we add an interrupt node for the RTC, so it is properly confgurable as an interrupt, and use a dummy handler.",
      "parentUuid": "66f7a562_ea310fa1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e172e8ce_3b1c4291",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T00:31:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could you file a bug for this broken code you mentioned? This RTC wake source used to be functional and we verified it, per b/193583152.\n\nThe RTC interrupt pin is configured in the RTC driver (shimmed), check http://b/194710429#comment8. Its interrupt handler is also inside the driver. It is not a function in the EC-OS, like most of the other GPIOs. Creating a dummy handler and following the EC-OS way seems duplicated. Will this dummy handler break the handler already registered by the driver?\n\nCC\u0027ed Sam",
      "parentUuid": "1d1b7b30_a932df3b",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "305514aa_4f062378",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T00:59:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I think part of the confusion here is the wrong naming of this pin.\nThe original name I think should be gpio_rts_ec_wake_odl instead of gpio_rtc_ec_wake_odl\nI just copied the name (hey, I\u0027m just following what\u0027s in the config already :-)\n\nThe wrong naming perhaps suggests that this pin is part of the RTC, when it is likely complete unrelated.\nAnd it appears that the RTC interrupt is enabled completely separately via system_set_rtc_alarm.\n\nI can rename the pin so that it matches trogdor. It does look like a typo.\nLooking at ec/board/lazor/gpio.inc, it says \"EC_RST_ODL\".",
      "parentUuid": "e172e8ce_3b1c4291",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c9ff90fa_2676930c",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T01:06:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, the plot thickens..\n\nherobrine has a gpio_rtc_ec_wake_odl, and it is set in the wake_pins, but this seems to be configured in the RTC driver, so it should not appear in the wake-pins.\nI can fix that.\n\nAlso, byra has this interesting bit of config in nct3808_alert_1.\nIt sets irq-gpios to GPIO e 0, but this GPIO is in named-gpios as usb_c0_c2_tcpc_int_odl\nOK, lots of brokenness here.",
      "parentUuid": "305514aa_4f062378",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d9b7a991_0ef27277",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T01:19:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So it appears herebrine has the only GPIO called gpio_rtc_ec_wake_odl, and it is actually for the RTC, and seems to be internally configured and handled separately to the other GPIOs, so IMHO it should not be included in the list of pins for wake-irq",
      "parentUuid": "c9ff90fa_2676930c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b856c80b_a7f32516",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T02:14:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The names of this RTC pin and the EC_RST_ODL are similar and easy to be confused 😊\n\nBTW, I still doubt the value of converting the GPIO config to the interrupt config. \n\nThe hibernate wake pin is not necessary to be an interrupt. The interrupt config is a wrapper of the GPIO. This conversion requires creating an extra wrapper even it is not necessary.\n \nThe code uses the `gpio_enable_interrupt(wakeup_pin_list[i]);` and treats the pins like interrupt. This is the only place the `hibernate-wake-pins` dt node is used. I doubt this code is just a no-op. Probably a copy of the legacy code from the EC-OS. As I recall that Sam discovered that modifying  `hibernate-wake-pins` node had no effect. Need to modify the `vsby-psl-in-list` node instead. Likely this `hibernate-wake-pins` and the related code can be totally removed. Just use the upstream driver way. Sam, do you have a bug for it?\n\nPlease give me some time to follow it up.",
      "parentUuid": "d9b7a991_0ef27277",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b4c3b01d_120e3a91",
        "filename": "zephyr/projects/trogdor/lazor/gpio.dts",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 345,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-01-29T02:33:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "OK np, I can only look at the code that is already there to try and\nfigure out the best thing to do. The intent of the code seems clear to me,\nthat the EC chip disables all interrupts and only enables interrupts for the pins listed in the wake-pins. If you say that the interrupts do not need to be enabled for these pins, then I am not sure why the code is doing that.\n\nPerhaps Sam was using one of the inputs that were not included in EC_CROS_GPIO_INTERRUPTS, and so was a no-op (see my comments earlier about why).\n\nSo my key goal here is to migrate away from gpio_enable_interrupt to\nthe zephyr API (which we wrap with gpio_enable_dt_interrupt).\n\nSo I hear your concern about converting wake-pins to wake-irq, and I am open to your suggestion about how we do it otherwise - the key problem here is if we just reference the GPIO how does the code in system_npcx_set_wakeup_gpios_before_hibernate know what interrupt flags to set (i.e GPIO_INT_ flags)?",
      "parentUuid": "b856c80b_a7f32516",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 338,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 345,
        "endChar": 6
      },
      "revId": "eebe3bd96279651aa6d313177705136013a83d62",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
